stereo-layer (1.17.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The passes hold the source's depth, and a way needs less of the GPU's memory (ORDER 411, step 2g; Daniel, 2026-10-07: video "at the
    source's depth (10/12/16 bits ...)", surfaces "at 10 bits or half float"; 2026-10-23: the efficient path to the screen by default).
    stereo_dibr_gl_set_precision(): the colour textures are half float for content of up to 10 bits and float above, never below the source;
    the cache of timings keeps 10 and 16 bit content apart. The colour textures of an 8 or 10 bit content are half float, the depth-sized
    textures of the parallax step, the splat and the band are handed back as soon as they are used, and the colour and state textures
    are made after that: the layered depth-aided way at 1080p per eye peaks at 193 MB counted from the passes (309 MB in 1.16.0), and the
    driver's free memory falls by 252 MB at its first drawing instead of 359 MB on a GeForce RTX 3060, by 234 MB instead of 332 MB on a
    Radeon R5 M330 (measured in a process of its own for each way).
  * stereo_dibr_way_memory(warp, fill, w, h, bits) counts the two-phase peak times what the driver adds (1.13 to 1.39 measured), and
    stereo_dibr_way_fits() is the one rule of the guard of the automatic choice and of the sentence beside a way in the controls: the
    layered depth-aided way at 1080p per eye fits beside a two-screen desktop on a 512 MB carve-out (289 MB free), the layered pyramid does
    not. The cheapest way is never held back by the guard. The controls say "needs about N MB of the GPU's memory, M MB is free" beside such
    a way in the fill and warp lists before it is picked (StereoDepthSettings::setGpuMemory(), wayText()); a way the person picks runs.
  * dibrtime: --memory and --only N; DIBRTIME_BITS sets the precision.

 -- Daniel Ramos (Capitain Jack) <Capitain_Jack@yahoo.com>  Thu, 05 Nov 2026 20:21:47 +0000

stereo-layer (1.16.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The GPU path is paced and watched (ORDER 411, step 2f; a GPU page fault and ring timeout on the station's Ryzen 5600G iGPU while the
    ladder was timed). The ladder is timed from the cheapest way up and stops at the first way that misses the frame's budget
    (stereo_dibr_time_ladder(); the ways are ordered by cost), so the ways that take seconds on a slow GPU never run during timing; a way
    eight times dearer than a cheaper one that took over 125 ms is not run either; the first way run, and every way above one that took
    over 20 ms, runs with its passes in bands of 270 rows, each finished before the next (stereo_dibr_gl_set_limits()). Ways not timed
    stay -1 in the cache and are timed when a lower frame rate leaves room (stereo_dibr_gl_measure() and stereo_dibr_measure_cpu() take
    the frame rate).
  * A way whose float textures (stereo_dibr_way_memory(): 150 bytes a pixel for layered depth-aided, 309 MB at 1080p per eye) are over
    half of what the driver says is free (GL_ATI_meminfo, GL_NVX_gpu_memory_info) is not run: -2 in the times, and the depth controls say
    "needs more of the GPU's memory than is free". Out of GPU memory is the likeliest cause of the fault (amdgpu said -12, ENOMEM, in its
    fault handler), but it was not reproduced: see REPORT-2f.md.
  * The context's reset status is watched (glGetGraphicsResetStatus, KHR_robustness): after every timed frame and every drawing; a reset
    ends the timing, the GL path refuses to draw (-4) and the controls say "the GPU was reset while drawing: the processor draws now".
    dibrtime creates a robust, debug context and prints KHR_debug messages, with --paced and --no-telea.
  * dibrchoosetest asserts the order and the stop of the timing on fixtures of times, the memory stop and the memory a way needs.

 -- Daniel Ramos (Capitain Jack) <Capitain_Jack@yahoo.com>  Thu, 05 Nov 2026 20:00:00 +0000

stereo-layer (1.15.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * A video is drawn the best way that keeps its frame rate (ORDER 411, group 2e; Daniel, 2026-11-05: "we choose the best default and
    left options to the user"). libstereo-dibr: stereo_dibr_ways() is the ladder of four ways that no other way beats at its cost
    (layered warping with depth-aided filling, layered with the pyramid fill, forward with the pyramid fill, forward with background
    filling), stereo_dibr_choose_from_times() takes the first that fits four fifths of the frame's time, stereo_dibr_gl_measure()
    and stereo_dibr_measure_cpu() time the ladder once for each GPU (or processor) and picture size, kept in the person's cache
    (XDG_CACHE_HOME/stereo-dibr/timings, mode 0600, never in a media file). The options carry whether the way is automatic or the
    person's (way=auto, way=own in the text); stills and the saving into a file use the best way, the options' default, which is
    also stereo_dibr_params_default(): the library has one default.
  * A new fill, the pyramid: the depth-aided idea (a hole is filled from the background around it, never from the foreground) in
    passes by halves of the picture, so that the work grows with the logarithm of the hole and not with its width. C and GLSL give
    the same views (dibrgltest: no pixel differs by more than one). Over both eyes its error is 0.746, depth-aided's 0.736 and
    background filling's 0.789 (layered warp, autotests/dibrbench.c); at 1920x1080 per eye on an RTX 3060 it takes 15 ms with the
    layered warp and 6.6 ms with the forward warp, against 72 and 52 ms for depth-aided.
  * Depth controls: Automatic (the way is chosen for a video), the way chosen and why in the person's language, the pyramid;
    picking a warping or a gap filling turns automatic off and the pick holds for everything, the slowest way included.
    dibrbench asserts that the ladder follows the measured ranking, dibrchoosetest asserts the choice on fixtures of times (the
    budget of a GPU) and the cache, dibrtime times every way on a GPU or the processor and, with --choose, what a player chooses.
  * Picture plus depth has one set of options for every program (ORDER 411, group 2c; Daniel, 2026-11-05: where the assembly of
    full side by side from a picture and its depth has options, the library carries the best default and the person's choice, and
    every player shows the same controls). libstereo-dibr: struct stereo_dibr_options (depth strength, screen plane, shift,
    which eye the picture is, warping, gap filling, smoothing, filling reach, the depth range), stereo_dibr_options_default(),
    _clamp(), _apply(), and the text a program keeps per file (_read, _write).
  * The default is the best way measured: autotests/dibrbench.c scores every way of drawing on scenes whose true views are known
    (layered shapes, exact and noisy depth, several depth budgets) and fails when the default is not the best. Layered warping with
    depth-aided filling has the lowest error over both eyes and where the picture shows nothing (0.736 and 4.14 against 0.806 and
    5.11 for forward warping with background filling, the earlier choice), smoothing the depth makes exact depth worse, and the
    default strength of 58 pixels is 1.03 degrees of visual angle. docs/depth-defaults.md has the table.
  * Controls in the library: Stereo::DepthSettings (QML StereoDepthSettings) holds the set; Stereo::DepthControls (Qt widgets) and
    DepthControls (QML, org.sparky.stereo) show it with the same words, translated in the eight edition languages.
  * libstereo-av, stereo_mark_save_views(): the size of the views of a side by side or top and bottom video goes into Matroska and
    WebM as DisplayWidth and DisplayHeight, "the number of pixels for one plane" (RFC 9559 section 18.10); MP4's st3d has no field
    for it and is refused (MP4_VIEWS), as is a layout with no such choice (VIEWS_LAYOUT). stereo_mark_read_detail() reads the
    sizes and whether the blocks lace two frames.
  * StereoMode 13 and 14 are both eyes laced in one Block (RFC 9559 sections 5.1.4.1.28.3 and 18.10): they are written only when
    the track's blocks are laced that way; frame sequential and block laced layouts into a file whose blocks are not laced are
    refused (NOT_LACED), though FFmpeg's muxer writes 13 and 14 for its frame sequence.
  * libstereo-policy: the depth of a display, one source for every program that puts depth on a stereo output.
    stereo_policy_display_depth() gives, per display kind (framed screen, anaglyph screen, headset), physical
    size and the person's choice (a strength in percent, or absolute limits), how far the output sinks and pops
    in pixels of a 1920 wide view and where it is seen from. The browser's WebXR modes read it, and Stereo KWin
    reads it for the desktop's depth, so a page and the desktop agree on the same screen (ORDER 194, docs/display-depth.md).
  * This entry also carries the work of 1.14.0, which was staged and never published: the depth options and their controls, the writers'
    size of the views and the laced blocks, and the yt3d reader and the 3D layout writers of 1.13.0.

 -- Daniel Ramos (Capitain Jack) <Capitain_Jack@yahoo.com>  Thu, 05 Nov 2026 17:00:00 +0000

stereo-layer (1.13.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * libstereo-policy: the site mark. stereo_policy_site_mark(), stereo_policy_site_mark_list()
    and stereo_policy_site_mark_word() read YouTube's yt3d keywords in every form
    (enable=LR, RL, LonR, RonL, true, false; swap; aspect; left and right
    rectangles; malformed values read as far as they can be), one reader for the
    browser and PlasmaTube (docs/yt3d.md). The first form is right eye first and
    swap=true makes it left eye first, as YouTube's player took them.
  * New in libstereo-av (stereo-mark.h): stereo_mark_save() writes a video's
    3D layout and eye order into its container when the person asked for it,
    so programs offer "save the correction into the file" through one library
    (ORDER 411, group 2b of ORDER 151). Matroska and WebM: the track's
    StereoMode, every value of RFC 9559 and the packed depth layouts 15 to 20
    IANA assigned on 26 October 2026, through mkvpropedit; WebM only 0, 1, 2,
    3 and 11 (WebM Container Guidelines). MP4 and MOV: st3d (Spherical Video
    V2: mono, top-bottom, left-right, right-left) written in place, so no chunk
    offset of ISO/IEC 14496-12 section 8.7.5 moves: into the last box, or into
    a free box after moov.
  * A depth layout into MP4 is refused (st3d has no depth value), as is every
    packing the container has no value for; a video whose stream declares
    another layout itself (the frame packing SEI) is refused; a file that
    carries the layout already is left alone. Every refusal is a status that
    says why, and leaves the file byte for byte.
  * Every write is read back by a reader of its own that follows the file's
    bytes (including a Tracks element mkvpropedit moved behind the clusters),
    and a write that does not read back is put back as it was.
  * The technique is that of sView's StVideoStereoMark (MIT), moved here so
    sView, Dragon Player and the players after them write the same way.
  * Test: marktest against ffmpeg-made files, with mkvmerge and libavformat as
    the independent readers, 516 checks.
  * Build-depend on ffmpeg and mkvtoolnix for that test. The programs that
    call stereo_mark_save depend on mkvtoolnix, which provides mkvpropedit.

